options.explanation:
$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(-\dfrac{5}{4y^3}\right)
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{5}{4}\cdot\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[y^{-3}\right]
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{5}{4}(-3)y^{-4}\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{15}{4y^4}\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}
\end{align}$

Now let's plug $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\dfrac{5}{4y^3}}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{15}{4y^4}\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{15}{4y^4}\cdot\left(\goldD{-\dfrac{5}{4y^3}}\right)
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{75}{16y^7}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Finally, let's plug $\blueD{x=2}$ and $\maroonD{y=1}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\left.-\dfrac{75}{16\maroonD y^7}\right\rvert_{(\blueD{2},\maroonD{1})}&=-\dfrac{75}{16(\maroonD{1})^7}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{75}{16}
\end{align}$

(Notice that the expression for $\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ only depends on $\maroonD y$ so there was no need to plug $\blueD{x=2}$.)
